<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kakusthavarma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 435–455</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Kakusthavarman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kakusthavarma was a king of the Kadamba dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banavasi">Banavasi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> in the 5th century, regarded as one of its most powerful rulers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talagunda-inscription">Talagunda inscription</a> records that he married his daughters into royal houses, including, according to later readings, the Gupta and Vakataka dynasties. Under him the Kadambas reached their greatest extent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kakusthavarma.jpg" alt="Kakusthavarma" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rani nurmai · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Kadamba_king_Sri_Dosharashi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
